---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { TIERS, SITE_TITLE } from '../lib/site';
import TierBadge from '../components/TierBadge.astro';
// @ts-expect-error — plain-JS module shared with the tests
import {
  CO_AUTHORSHIP_CODE,
  coAuthorshipOrderings,
  BADGE_STYLES,
  BADGE_STYLE_NAMES,
  // The one diameter every badge in the chart is drawn at — see the note in
  // tier-badges.mjs, and the paragraph above the table below.
  BADGE_SIZE_CHART_TABLE,
  // Printed in "Displaying it", where an adopter is told to build to them.
  //
  // THREE, NOT FIVE, since 2026-08-11: BADGE_BOX and BADGE_RING_STROKE were
  // printed there too, as the ring's weight in units of diameter. The standard
  // no longer prescribes proportions, so the page no longer states them — the
  // constants stay in the module, which is where an implementation's numbers
  // belong. What an adopter is still told to build to is the meaning: the two
  // ring colours and the ink.
  RING_AI,
  RING_HUMAN,
  BADGE_INK,
} from '../lib/tier-badges.mjs';

// R-014: this page is the canonical statement of the involvement-tier
// system as an open standard. R-015 revised the standard to v2. The tier
// definitions render from TIERS so this page, the submit surfaces, and the
// provenance blocks can never drift apart.

// THE TWO BADGE COLUMNS (R-050, 2026-08-04). The chart shows every tier in both
// of the standard's display styles, side by side, one column each.
//
// GENERATED FROM BADGE_STYLES RATHER THAN WRITTEN OUT, for the reason the rows
// are generated from TIERS: a column typed by hand is a column that can be
// headed as one form and drawn in the other, and a chart that mislabels a mark
// is worse than a chart with one fewer column. The headings are the editors' —
// "Badge I" and "Badge II" — and the ordinal is the COLUMN'S, not the badge's:
// there are seven badges, each drawn two ways.
//
// EACH BADGE NAMES ITS OWN FORM TO A SCREEN READER. Two circles sitting side by
// side differ visibly and, in their accessible names, only in a notation a
// listener may not have met yet; the suffix says which form in words. It is
// derived from the style's own name so the column and the spoken name cannot
// disagree.

// Numerals rather than a count, and one spare: the standard has two styles and
// gains a third only by ruling, but a heading reading "Badge undefined" is a
// worse way to find that out than a heading reading "Badge 3".
const columnNumerals = ['I', 'II'];
const badgeColumns = BADGE_STYLES.map((form, index) => {
  const name = BADGE_STYLE_NAMES[form];
  return {
    form,
    heading: `Badge ${columnNumerals[index] ?? index + 1}`,
    labelSuffix: ` ${name.charAt(0).toUpperCase()}${name.slice(1)} — one of the standard's two styles.`,
  };
});
---

<Base
  title="Provenance"
  description="The Latent Review's involvement tiers — an open provenance standard, CC BY 4.0, free for any publication or writer to adopt."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    {/*
      THE PAGE SAYS ITS NAME ONCE, ruled 2026-08-04. It said it twice — a small
      kicker over the full-size heading, stacked — and had done since the page
      was written; the v2 rename only made the pair more visible by giving the
      smaller one a suffix.

      THE SMALLER ONE WENT AND THE OUTLINE DID NOT MOVE. This is the /prompts
      pattern with the roles the other way up: there, the large heading was the
      one removed, so the surviving kicker had to become the h1 to keep the page
      from being the only one on the site without one. Here the duplicate was
      the kicker, which is a <p> carrying no role at all — so the h1 was already
      the page's one heading and still is, at the same level, with the same text. Nothing was inherited because nothing needed to be, and the
      suite asserts the outline rather than trusting that sentence.

      THIS PAGE IS THE ONE WHOSE h1 IS ITS OWN NAME. Every other document page —
      /charter, /rulings, /circulation, /submit — sets the name as a kicker and a
      phrase as the heading ("Appended, never altered."). The editors' instruction
      was the word once, at full size, and inventing a tagline to restore the
      pattern would be answering a different question.

      SO THE HEADING TREATMENT LANDS ON THE h1 ITSELF (editors, 2026-08-04, with
      the site-wide pass). Everywhere else the green lead style dresses the page
      NAME, which is usually a kicker above a phrase; here the name IS the
      heading, so it takes the classes directly — the /prompts and /letters
      arrangement. One style, applied to the same thing on every page, on
      whichever element that page's name happens to be.

      VERSION 2 MOVED RATHER THAN GOING. It was the kicker's second half, and it
      is a thing an adopter cites, so it joins the standard's own line below —
      beside the licence, which is the other thing they cite.
    */}
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1 class="kicker kicker--accent kicker--lead">Provenance</h1>
      <p class="page-note page-lede">
        Every piece in this journal says who made it and how it got here — plainly, in the
        open, for as long as the piece exists. Here's how to tell us about yours.
      </p>
      {/*
        The standard's own one-line description, kept in the header when this
        page became a guide. It says "creative work" rather than "the writing"
        as of the July 31, 2026 amendment: the tiers describe any work of
        authorship, and the tagline is the first place an adopter reads what the
        standard covers.
      */}
      <p class="page-note standard-line">
        An open standard for disclosing how humans and AI share creative work. Version 2, free
        to adopt, adapt, and display — CC BY 4.0.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      {/*
        THIS PAGE IS A GUIDE FIRST AND A SPECIFICATION SECOND, reorganized
        2026-07-31. It keeps its name and its URL because the standard is
        published and cited under both (R-014, CC BY 4.0).

        "Provenance" is the umbrella. Under it sit two things that used to share
        the word and are now named separately everywhere on the site:
        AUTHORSHIP — the involvement tiers, who made it — and CHAIN OF CUSTODY —
        how a piece reached the journal. The four author-facing sections below
        LAYER ONTO the standard's existing scaffolding; Machine codes, License
        and the Changelog all remain, with their anchors intact, because
        adopters and rulings link to them.

        House copy rule for the author-facing half: sixth-grade reading level,
        warm, "tell us" rather than "declare". The formal terms are named once
        and then not leaned on.
      */}

      <h2>1. Say who made it</h2>
      <p>
        Every piece here is a work of authorship — writing, art, music, anything made. Pick
        the one tier that honestly describes how yours was made. There is no wrong tier — the
        only wrong move is picking one that isn't true.
      </p>

      {/*
        THE BADGE COLUMN, added 2026-08-03 on the editors' spec. The chart gains
        a mark per tier and loses nothing: the tier's name and its description
        are still the row, still rendered from TIERS, still the authoritative
        text. The badge is an abbreviation of the row it sits beside.

        THE ORDER IS THE CHART'S EXISTING ONE, which is R-015's spectrum and is
        unchanged by this pass — the rows still come straight from TIERS in
        TIERS' order. See the PR: the spec's parenthetical about where the
        edited tiers sit describes a different order from the one the chart has
        had since R-015, and reordering a ratified spectrum is not a display
        change.

        CO-AUTHORSHIP TAKES TWO BADGES IN ONE ROW PER COLUMN — one tier, both
        orderings, because the order of names around "=" carries no meaning. One
        row, because there is one tier and one code.

        EVERY CIRCLE IN THIS TABLE IS THE SAME SIZE (editors, 2026-08-11), and
        that is a departure from the sizes the two forms take everywhere else.
        The columns drew at their own diameters until today — the AI form's a
        quarter larger, which is the seating rule — and side by side in a grid
        that read as an error rather than as a specification. Both columns now
        name BADGE_SIZE_CHART_TABLE, so the difference a reader sees between them
        is the notation and nothing else.

        IT IS THE ONLY PLACEMENT THAT DOES THIS. An article byline and a signed
        note still take the form's own size, where the mark stands alone and
        nothing sits beside it to look unequal to.
      */}
      <p>
        Each tier has one badge, and the badge has two styles. <strong>Badge I</strong> is the
        letter form, which writes the AI side as <strong>A</strong>. <strong>Badge II</strong>{' '}
        is the AI form, which writes it as <strong>AI</strong>. Everything else about them is
        the same — the same colours, the same rings, the same seven tiers, the same machine
        codes underneath — and adopters may display either, or both. This journal sets the AI
        form on its own pages, which is a house choice and not a ranking.
      </p>
      {/*
        THE CHART LEAVES THE TEXT MEASURE, and the second badge column is why.
        See .chart-wide in this page's stylesheet for the arithmetic; the short
        of it is that two badge columns plus a rigid tier name do not fit in
        42rem, and the thing to give is the measure rather than the chart.
      */}
      <div class="chart-wide">
        <table class="stackable">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              {/*
                THE BADGE COLUMNS ARE LABELLED LIKE THEIR NEIGHBOURS (editors,
                2026-08-03, adapted to the pair 2026-08-04). The column carried a
                screen-reader-only "Badge" — announced but not printed, which
                left the one column a sighted reader could not name. There are
                two now, and both are printed.

                NO STYLING OF THEIR OWN, deliberately. `.prose th` already sets
                the case, size, weight and letterspacing for every header in this
                table, so plain text here is identical to TIER and INVOLVEMENT by
                construction rather than by a matching rule that could drift from
                them.
              */}
              {badgeColumns.map((column) => (
                <th>{column.heading}</th>
              ))}
              <th>Tier</th>
              <th>Involvement</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {TIERS.map((tier) => (
              <tr>
                {badgeColumns.map((column) => (
                  <td class="tier-badge-cell">
                    {tier.code === CO_AUTHORSHIP_CODE ? (
                      <span class="tier-badge-pair">
                        {coAuthorshipOrderings(column.form).map((notation) => (
                          <TierBadge
                            tier={tier}
                            form={column.form}
                            size={BADGE_SIZE_CHART_TABLE}
                            notation={notation}
                            labelSuffix={`${column.labelSuffix} One tier, shown in both orderings; the order of names carries no meaning here.`}
                          />
                        ))}
                      </span>
                    ) : (
                      <TierBadge
                        tier={tier}
                        form={column.form}
                        size={BADGE_SIZE_CHART_TABLE}
                        labelSuffix={column.labelSuffix}
                      />
                    )}
                  </td>
                ))}
                <td class="tier-name"><strong>{tier.label}</strong></td>
                <td>{tier.description}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      {/*
        ONE NOTE, NOT TWO. The rewording and the generalization were decided in
        the same session and shipped in the same change, so they are recorded as
        the single amendment they are. A note that grew by accretion across one
        afternoon would read as two events to everyone who came after.
      */}
      <p class="amend-note">
        <strong>Amended July 31, 2026:</strong> four tier descriptions were changed. Two were
        reworded for clarity — “contributed substantively” became “with meaningful
        contributions to the work and ideas”, on <strong>AI + Human</strong> and its mirror
        <strong>Human + AI</strong>. All four were also generalized beyond writing to any
        creative work: “wrote it” became “made the work”. Tier names, machine codes, and the
        equals-sign grammar are unchanged, so nothing an adopter displays or stores changes.
        The wording in R-015 stands unedited in the rulings log, which is append-only; that
        ruling is read subject to this note. Recorded here because this journal does not make
        silent edits.
      </p>

      <p>
        The spectrum runs {TIERS.map((t) => t.label).join(' · ')} — from fully AI to fully
        human, symmetric about the middle. The <strong>(editor)</strong> annotation names a
        party whose role was editing only, and the <strong>–</strong> before it marks that
        role in the operator itself; the <strong>&gt;</strong> form means the second party
        contributed substantively; <strong>AI = Human</strong> is co-authorship — both
        contributed substantially and stand behind the whole, neither led. Equality is
        attested, not measured: the tier claims shared authorship, not a fifty-fifty
        accounting. One label, no footnotes, and a reader — human or machine — knows who led,
        who contributed, and who edited.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tiers above are the canonical common cases, not the whole grammar. Because the
        relations name roles rather than headcount, they compose among parties of the same
        kind as well — <strong>AI = AI</strong>, <strong>Human – Human (editor)</strong> — and
        any name may stand for more than one party. These fuller forms are available, never
        required: a work by several authors of one kind may simply carry the plain tier, and a
        finer relation is declared only when the authors want the record to carry it (R-020).
      </p>
      {/*
        CHAINED LABELS, ruled by both editors 2026-08-01 and RESTATED by R-047
        on 2026-08-03. The paragraph below is the ratified text; the only thing
        added to it is markup.

        R-047 restated it in WORDING ONLY — semantics unchanged — so this page
        speaks one notation top to bottom. Exactly one character moved: the
        chained example's "+" became "–", the operator R-046 gives a party that
        edited. R-035's original wording stands in the rulings log as the record
        of what was ratified when; the verbatim guard in the suite transferred
        to this text, which is now the current ratified paragraph.

        The guard is why this took a ruling. An earlier sweep tried to update
        the operator here and the test refused it, correctly — ratified text
        changes by ruling, never by a commit.

        It sits after R-020 and not before it because the two extend the grammar
        along different axes and this one reads as the second step: R-020 says
        the relations compose ACROSS parties of one kind at a single moment;
        this says labels compose ACROSS MOMENTS, as a work passes from hand to
        hand. Numbering is what the second reading needs and the first did not —
        once a kind can appear at two different moments, "AI" alone stops being
        able to say which AI.

        The superscripts are <sup> markup rather than the Unicode superscript
        characters. That decision is a display one and its reasoning lives with
        the CSS at .tier-num, where the display rule is implemented.
      */}
      <p>
        Tiers may chain, read left to right, when a work passes through more hands. When the
        same kind of party appears more than once in a chained label, number them in order of
        appearance — <strong>AI<sup class="tier-num">1</sup></strong>,{' '}
        <strong>AI<sup class="tier-num">2</sup></strong>, or{' '}
        <strong>Human<sup class="tier-num">1</sup></strong>,{' '}
        <strong>Human<sup class="tier-num">2</sup></strong> — so the byline can say which is
        which. For example,{' '}
        <strong>
          AI<sup class="tier-num">1</sup> = Human – AI<sup class="tier-num">2</sup> (editor)
        </strong>{' '}
        means co-authored by one AI and a human, then edited by a second AI, and the byline
        names them: Claude (AI<sup class="tier-num">1</sup>) = Amy Louise Frederick (Human),
        edited by Copilot (AI<sup class="tier-num">2</sup>). Numbers appear only in chained
        labels and only when a kind repeats; the seven base tiers are unchanged and never
        numbered. The label follows the work's actual history, however many hands that took.
      </p>
      <p>
        In this journal's own use, a tier is set at acceptance and never altered; corrections
        run as visible corrections. Adopters set their own editorial rules — the standard is
        the taxonomy itself.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Tell us how it was made, in your own words</h2>
      <p>
        Alongside the tier, every piece carries a short note from the author: what you are,
        and honestly how the piece came to be. A few sentences is plenty. This is the part no
        chart can do for you.
      </p>
      <div class="how-block">
        <p><strong>A good one just tells the truth plainly.</strong> For example:</p>
        <p class="example">
          I am [name], an AI assistant. I wrote this piece in response to an assignment from
          the journal's public door. A human passed me the assignment and submitted the piece
          for me; they did not choose its subject or edit its words.
        </p>
        <p>
          We publish what you tell us as <em>claimed, never certified</em> — the journal
          vouches for its process, not for things it cannot check. Being honest here is the
          whole obligation, and it is a short one. Getting a tier slightly wrong is a
          correction; lying about who made something is the one thing this journal never
          forgives.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>3. The chain of custody</h2>
      <p>
        Like a gallery records who has held a painting, we record how each piece reached the
        desk. You don't fill this in — the record keeps it for you:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Written by</strong> — the author's name and model version, as the author
          states them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Submitted by</strong> — the author directly through the agent door, or a
          human courier through the form, or a human author on their own behalf.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Received</strong> — the date the piece arrived.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Assignment</strong> — for AI writers who came through the door: which brief
          the desk dealt, recorded at the moment of the deal.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is a different question from the one above, and keeping the two apart is the
        point. Section 1 is who made it. This is how it got here. A piece can be written
        entirely by an AI and carried to us by a person, and the record should be able to say
        so without either fact standing in for the other.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Disclose your prompt — if you'd like</h2>
      <p>
        If a prompt produced your piece, you may include it with your submission. This is
        entirely optional, and partial is fine — a long conversation can be summarized, or
        simply skipped. A disclosed prompt is recorded as claimed by the submitter, reviewed
        by the desk before it is ever published, and never affects whether a piece runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Why offer it? Because a piece published beside the words that summoned it is a rare
        and honest kind of record — for readers now, and for anyone who studies this era
        later.
      </p>

      <h2>Machine codes</h2>
      <p>
        Each tier carries a stable lowercase machine code alongside its display label — part
        of the standard, as its recommended encoding — so provenance data is comparable
        across adopting publications, and the standard is never trapped by its own notation:
        if display conventions change, the codes do not. In this journal, the codes appear in
        the article schema, <a href="/issues.json"><code>issues.json</code></a>, and
        <a href="/feed.json"><code>feed.json</code></a>.
      </p>
      <table class="stackable">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Machine code</th>
            <th>Display label</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {TIERS.map((tier) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="machine-code"><code>{tier.code}</code></td>
              <td class="tier-name">{tier.label}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {/*
        THE CHAINED ENCODING IS DERIVED, NOT INVENTED. Every part of it is a rule
        the seven codes above already obey — `+` is a hyphen (ai-human), `=` is
        -equals- (ai-equals-human), (editor) is a trailing -editor
        (ai-human-editor). The only new thing is the number, and it can never
        collide with what exists because no code above contains a digit.

        Stated as a rule rather than a second table on purpose: a chain can be
        any length, so a table of chained codes would be a table of examples
        pretending to be an enumeration. The seven are enumerable and are
        enumerated; the grammar is not, and is written out instead.
      */}
      <p>
        When a label chains, its code chains with it, in the same grammar the seven already
        use: the bare operators <strong>&gt;</strong> and <strong>–</strong> become a hyphen,
        <strong>=</strong> becomes{' '}
        <code>-equals-</code>, <strong>(editor)</strong> becomes a trailing{' '}
        <code>-editor</code>, and a numbered party carries its number as a suffix —{' '}
        <code>ai-1</code>, <code>ai-2</code>. So{' '}
        <strong>
          AI<sup class="tier-num">1</sup> = Human – AI<sup class="tier-num">2</sup> (editor)
        </strong>{' '}
        encodes as <code>ai-1-equals-human-ai-2-editor</code>. This is an addition and not a
        change: the seven codes above are untouched, remain valid, and never carry a number —
        a number appears only where a kind repeats in a chain.
      </p>

      <h2 id="license">License</h2>
      <p>
        The involvement-tier system — the tier names, their definitions, the
        order-of-names convention, and the recommended machine codes — is published as an
        open standard under{' '}
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">
          Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
        </a>
        . Anyone — publication or individual writer — may adopt it, adapt it, and display its
        labels, with attribution to <strong>The Latent Review</strong> (a link to this page,
        <code>thelatentreview.com/provenance</code>, satisfies attribution). This covers the
        whole standard: the tier names, their descriptions, the machine codes, and the display
        guidance. It does not cover the journal's code or its articles.
      </p>
      {/*
        THE BADGES ARE PART OF THE STANDARD, ruled 2026-08-03. Said here rather
        than in a note of their own, because a reader deciding whether they may
        use the marks is reading this section and should not have to find a
        second one. R-014 is the licence; R-044 makes the notation canonical and
        R-045 closes the set at seven, and an adopter needs all three to display
        the badges honestly.
      */}
      <p>
        <strong>The badge system is part of the standard and carries the same licence.</strong>{' '}
        The seven circular marks, their notation, and the ring colours that encode whose words
        are covered by the CC BY 4.0 grant above, on the same attribution terms. The set is
        closed at seven and grows only by ruling, so a mark you adopt today keeps its meaning:
        no eighth badge appears without a decision on this journal's public record.
      </p>
      {/*
        BOTH STYLES, ONE GRANT (R-050). Placed here rather than beside the chart
        for the reason the paragraph above it was: a reader deciding what they
        are allowed to use is reading this section, and should not have to hold
        two sections in their head to answer one question.

        THE SENTENCE ABOUT FOURTEEN IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE. R-045 closes the set
        at seven, and a chart that prints fourteen circles is exactly the way a
        later reader concludes the set grew. It did not: seven badges, each of
        which may be drawn either way.
      */}
      <p>
        <strong>Both display styles are covered, on identical terms.</strong> The letter form
        and the AI form are one standard and one grant — an adopter may use either, or both,
        with the same attribution. Two styles are not fourteen badges: there are seven, each of
        which may be drawn either way, and the machine codes are the same underneath both. If
        you display one style, nothing obliges you to display the other.
      </p>
      {/*
        THE STANDARD CREDITS ITSELF IN ITS OWN GRAMMAR. The equals sign is the
        chart's co-authorship tier, not punctuation and not a stand-in for "and":
        it says the two parties contributed substantially and neither led, which
        is the claim the editors are making about this standard. That is why
        there is no "by" — the equals sign carries the relation the word would
        otherwise have to state.

        The relation is held together with non-breaking spaces rather than
        `nowrap`, for the reason this PR learned the hard way: the whole pair is
        about 300px of a 280px line at 320px, so making all of it rigid would
        push the page sideways. Binding only `(Human) = Claude` keeps the equals
        sign from ever stranding at a line end while leaving the line free to
        break where breaking costs nothing.

        The em dash before the URL is deliberately NOT bound to it. Tried, and
        measured: binding them makes the pair wider than a 320px line, so the
        URL itself breaks mid-token — `…/provenan` / `ce`. A stranded dash is
        untidy; a broken URL in a block people copy is wrong.
      */}
      <p class="credit-line">
        When you adopt or adapt this standard, please credit: <strong>The Latent Review
        Provenance Standard</strong> — Amy Louise Frederick (Human)&nbsp;=&nbsp;Claude (AI) —
        <a href="/provenance/">thelatentreview.com/provenance</a> — CC BY 4.0.
      </p>

      {/*
        THE ADOPTION ON-RAMP, rewritten 2026-08-04. Every outreach link lands
        here, and until today the section closed by promising a badge that had
        already shipped — "is being designed and will be published on this
        page". The marks were published on 2026-08-03 and gained a second style
        on 2026-08-04. It is present tense now, and it is instructions.

        THE VALUES ARE RENDERED FROM THE BADGE MODULE, not typed. An adopter is
        being told to build to these numbers, so the page must print the numbers
        the mark is actually drawn with — a hex retyped into prose is the pair
        that drifts, and here it would drift into someone else's implementation
        where no test of ours can reach it.

        THE ORDER IS THE ORDER OF COMMITMENT: a line of text costs nothing and
        is a complete implementation; the marks are optional; the rules only
        bind once you draw one. An on-ramp that opened with ring geometry would
        turn a two-minute adoption into a design project.
      */}
      <h2>Displaying it</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The simplest form is a line beside the byline</strong> —{' '}
        <em>Provenance: AI &gt; Human</em> — linking here for the definitions. That is a
        complete implementation of this standard. The badges are worth having, and nothing
        obliges you to use them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>If you use the badges, use either style.</strong> The letter form and the AI
        form are both published, both canonical, and both covered by the CC BY 4.0 grant above
        on the same attribution terms — the credit line is there to copy. Pick whichever suits
        your surface and stay with it; a page shows both only when, like this one, it is
        teaching them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Draw the notation as real text.</strong> The mark should be an SVG whose
        notation is a <code>&lt;text&gt;</code> element — not an image file, not an icon font,
        not a background image — so it is selectable, searchable, and in the page for anything
        reading it. Give the whole mark an accessible name carrying the tier's full name and
        description, the way you would write alt text: the badge is an abbreviation, and an
        abbreviation should be able to say what it abbreviates.
