        <strong>The ring is where the meaning is, so take the colours from it.</strong> Green{' '}
        <code>{RING_AI.toUpperCase()}</code> is the AI side; salmon{' '}
        <code>{RING_HUMAN.toUpperCase()}</code> is the human side; the notation inside is set in{' '}
        <code>{BADGE_INK.toUpperCase()}</code>. Co-authorship alone takes
        a ring split down the middle, drawn as two arcs so the halves are exactly equal, and
        the colour on the left matches the party on the left — <strong>AI = Human</strong>{' '}
        reads green then salmon, <strong>Human = AI</strong> the other way round.
      </p>
      {/*
        THE STANDARD STOPPED PRESCRIBING SIZE (editors, 2026-08-11). This
        paragraph told an adopter the ring's weight as a ratio, told them to
        scale the drawing as a unit, and told them the AI form's circle is a
        quarter larger than the letter form's with its notation a quarter
        smaller — three prescriptions about proportion, in a standard whose
        subject is notation, tier meaning and form.

        SIZING WAS NEVER THE POINT, and a standard that specifies it is asking
        an adopter to fit their design to ours. What a badge must do is say
