<style>
  /* THE CHART LEAVES THE TEXT MEASURE (R-050, 2026-08-04), and it is the only
     thing on this page that does.

     THE ARITHMETIC, because a breakout is the kind of rule that gets deleted by
     someone who reads it as decoration. At 0.95rem the chart's rigid content is:
     co-authorship's pair in each column, two 72.5px marks and a gap, 153px, plus
     28px of padding, twice — 362px — and the tier name, which is nowrap by the
     rule below, about 169px. That is 531px before a single word of the
     description column. The prose measure is 42rem and gives 632px of content,
     which would leave the description 101px — about eleven characters, running
     every tier to seven lines.

     THE NUMBER WAS 567px UNTIL 2026-08-11, when the two columns were levelled to
     one diameter and the AI pair came down from 189px to 153px. The breakout
     survives the change on its own arithmetic: 531px of rigid content still does
     not leave a readable description inside 42rem, so the measure still gives.

     So one of the four columns had to give, and the measure is the right one:
     the rigid widths are all ruled — the badge sizes by R-049 and R-050, the
     unbroken tier name by the rule below — and a text measure is a preference
     about reading comfort that a table full of marks is not bound by. The chart
     is the one element on the page that is not prose.

     IT CANNOT SCROLL THE PAGE SIDEWAYS. The width is the smaller of 52rem and
     the viewport less its gutters, and the pull-out is derived from the same
     min() — so on any viewport narrower than the cap the rule degrades to
     centring the chart in the measure it already had, and the negative margin
     reaches exactly zero rather than going past it. */
     THE CAP IS WRITTEN THE SAME WAY TWICE, and it has to be: the width and the
     pull are two halves of one number, and a cap edited in one of them would
     centre the chart somewhere other than where it sits. The suite asserts the
     two expressions match. */
  .chart-wide {
    width: min(52rem, 100vw - 2.5rem);
    margin-inline: calc((min(52rem, 100vw - 2.5rem) - 100%) / -2);
  }

  /* The badge columns: each as narrow as its mark plus breathing room, so the
     tier name still starts near the left edge and the chart still reads as a
     table of words with marks beside them. Both marks are 72.5px as of
     2026-08-11 — the AI column drew at 90.625px between August 4 and then — and
     co-authorship's pair is twice the mark plus a gap. */
  .tier-badge-cell {
    width: 1%;
    white-space: nowrap;
    padding-right: 1rem;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  /* Co-authorship's two badges sit side by side and wrap together on a narrow
     screen rather than splitting across the row. */
  .tier-badge-pair {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* This page had no scoped styles: it rendered entirely through global .prose.
     These are additions for the guide half only — the standard's own sections
     keep the shared typography they always had. */

  .page-lede {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  /* The standard's tagline, set under the guide lede rather than beside it:
     the first line greets an author, the second tells an adopter what they are
     allowed to take. Two audiences, one header, in that order. */
  .standard-line {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* A TIER NAME IS ONE UNIT AND NEVER BREAKS.
     "(editor)" is part of the label — it names what the second party did — and
     when the column was narrow enough to wrap it, "AI + Human" sat on one line
     with "(editor)" beneath, which reads as a footnote qualifying the tier
     rather than as part of its name. Measured before the fix at 320px: the two
     (editor) labels broke onto THREE lines, and every compound label broke onto
     two; both (editor) labels still broke at 768px, so this was never only a
     phone problem.

     Applied to both tables — the chart and the machine-code table — because the
     label renders in a column in each.

     ON ITS OWN THIS RULE OVERFLOWS THE PAGE, which is why the media query below
     exists rather than being tidiness. A rigid 188px label in a 280px line left
     the description column unable to reach its own minimum, and the page
     scrolled sideways by 42px at 320px. Measured, not predicted. */
  .tier-name {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The consequence of the line above, in the machine-code table: with the
     label column now rigid, the code column is what gives, and `.prose code`
     carries `overflow-wrap: anywhere` — so `ai-human-editor` would break
     mid-token into four lines at 320px. A machine code is a single identifier
     that people copy; breaking it anywhere is worse than the wrap this PR set
     out to fix. It breaks only at its own hyphens now, and nowhere else. */
  .machine-code code {
    overflow-wrap: normal;
    word-break: keep-all;
  }

  /* The numbered superscript's styling (R-035 clause 3) is GLOBAL, in
     src/styles/global.css. It was scoped here when this page was the only
     surface that rendered a numbered label; it is not any more, and a display
     rule the standard states cannot live inside one page's stylesheet. */

  /* BELOW 24rem THE TABLE STOPS BEING A TABLE, because at that width it stopped
     being readable as one. Measured at 320px with the labels made rigid: the
     name column wants 188px of a 280px line, leaving 92px — about ten
     characters — for the description, which then ran to seven lines. The rigid
     label was also 42px wider than the line, so the page itself scrolled
     sideways.

     Two columns is the wrong shape for a 320px screen, and the honest fix is
     not to shrink the type until it fits: it is to stack. The tier name sits on
     its own line, the description beneath it, and both get the full measure.
     Nothing is hidden, nothing scrolls, and the reading order is the one the
     table already implied. The header row is dropped because "Tier /
     Involvement" over a stacked pair labels nothing.

     This is the only media query in the codebase. The layout is otherwise
     fluid, and it stays that way — the breakpoint exists because a rigid label
     has a width that fluidity cannot argue with.

     MOVED 24rem → 30rem ON 2026-08-03, because the badges grew a quarter and
     the rigid width grew with them. Co-authorship's cell is two 72.5px marks
     and a gap — 153px, up from 124px — and between the old breakpoint and about
     30rem that left the tier name and the description sharing what was left of
     a phone's measure. The badges are the reason the number moved, so the
     number moves with them rather than the chart being allowed to crush its own
     text.

     MOVED 30rem → 44rem ON 2026-08-04, for the same reason at a larger size: a
     whole second badge column arrived. The rigid content was 567px (the
     arithmetic is at .chart-wide above), and the threshold both previous moves
     were set at is about 90–100px of description before the table stops being
     readable as one. 567 + 100 = 667px of table, which the breakout gives at a
     viewport of about 44rem. The number is derived from the same rule as the two
     before it, not chosen.

     LEFT AT 44rem ON 2026-08-11, when levelling the two columns took the rigid
     content down to 531px and the same rule would now put the threshold at about
     40rem. The number is not moved back, and the reason is which way the error
     runs: 44rem stacks a table that would still have fitted, which costs a reader
     between 40 and 44rem a layout they did not need, while 40rem risks the
     crushed description every previous move was made to prevent. A breakpoint
     that is four rem conservative is a worse layout for nobody. */
  @media (max-width: 44rem) {
    .stackable,
    .stackable tbody,
    .stackable tr,
    .stackable td {
      display: block;
      width: auto;
    }

    .stackable thead {
      display: none;
    }

    .stackable tr {
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
      padding: 0.55rem 0;
    }

    .stackable td {
      border-bottom: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    /* The name leads its pair, so it carries the space that separates one tier
       from the next rather than sitting flush against its own description. */
    .stackable td.tier-name,
    .stackable td.machine-code {
      margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
    }

    /* THE TWO FORMS STAY SIDE BY SIDE EVEN WHEN THE TABLE STOPS BEING A TABLE.
       Stacked, every other cell becomes a block; these two do not, because the
       whole claim of the pair is that it is one tier drawn two ways, and a
       reader who has to scroll from the first to the second is comparing a mark
       with a memory of a mark.

       inline-block rather than a flex row, so it degrades the honest way: the
       two sit on one line where the line holds them, and the AI form drops
       below the letter form where it does not — which on a 320px screen is
       co-authorship's four circles and nothing else. */
    .stackable td.tier-badge-cell {
      display: inline-block;
      margin-right: 0.6rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
    }
  }

  /* The dated amendment note. Set apart rather than folded into body text
     because it is a record of a change, not part of the standard's argument. */
  .amend-note {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--hairline);
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* The requested credit, set as a block an adopter can lift whole. Given its
     own box rather than run into the license paragraph because it is the thing
     someone came to this section to copy, and prose around it makes them work
     out where the credit stops. */
  .credit-line {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--hairline);
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  /* The worked example, set apart so a writer can see at a glance what is being
     asked of them without first reading the standard around it. */
  .how-block {
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    /* A rule, not type — the ring green itself, per the two stops in
       global.css. On this page especially: the mark it echoes is overhead. */
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-bright);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.4rem;
  }

  .how-block > p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .example {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
